cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Now before North plays, note another suit combination that is good to know how play. If clubs are 3-2 then North has 4 club tricks. But if clubs are 4-1, based on the double, East is more likely to have 4 clubs. Obviously the declarer, North here, does not know whether the Clubs are 3-2 or 4-1 or different as you see here.|
PG| ***** 93 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Many players forget about the auction when they plan the play, but they shouldn't. The declarer should base the line of play on the auction as in the case based on the double--4-1 likely.|
PG| ***** 94 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|With that information in order to get the maximum number of tricks North should play a club to the Ace and a club back to the Queen and can then finesse the 10 if need be.|
PG| ***** 95 ***** |


